# Fixtures are BYTES, and their digests are the point.
#
# `artifact_v1.tsv` is pinned by a sha256 recorded inside `history_v1.jsonl` when the v1
# code wrote it. Git's default on Windows is `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites LF to
# CRLF on checkout — so the file arrives with different bytes, a different hash, and the
# lineage join it exists to prove silently finds nothing. Windows CI caught exactly that.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions while keeping the files
# diffable. Anything whose hash is asserted belongs here.
tests/fixtures/** -text

# AND THE CROSS-VERSION CORPUS, which is the same rule reaching a second place — this file
# carried a list of one directory, a second directory of digest-asserted bytes arrived, and
# the list did not grow. The scope pattern, in the file that documents the scope pattern.
#
# `tests/corpus/**` is worse than the fixtures if it goes wrong: the artifacts carry pins that
# `verify` re-derives, and the environment snapshot's FILENAME IS the digest of its body, so a
# LF->CRLF checkout does not merely break a join — it makes every corpus artifact report STALE
# and every snapshot report a digest that does not describe it, on Windows only.
#
# A test now asserts that every file under both directories has text conversion unset, so a
# third directory of asserted bytes cannot arrive unprotected the way this one did.
tests/corpus/** -text
